1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,100
Stop eating for sixteen hours, says the

2
00:00:02,330 --> 00:00:05,280
internet, and something
switches on inside you.

3
00:00:05,749 --> 00:00:07,949
Your cells start taking out the trash.

4
00:00:08,369 --> 00:00:11,909
Old, damaged parts get
broken down and recycled.

5
00:00:12,589 --> 00:00:14,549
A kind of built-in repair mode.

6
00:00:15,215 --> 00:00:20,035
It even has a scientific name.
Autophagy. Greek for "self-eating".

7
00:00:20,966 --> 00:00:26,086
And in 2016, the scientist who worked
out how it happens won a Nobel Prize.

8
00:00:26,929 --> 00:00:29,729
But where does that
sixteen actually come from?

9
00:00:30,169 --> 00:00:32,869
And has anyone ever measured
it in a human being?

10
00:00:33,691 --> 00:00:37,091
Follow the number back, and the
trail gets thin very quickly.

11
00:00:37,733 --> 00:00:40,493
Sixteen hours is familiar
from a meal schedule.

12
00:00:40,953 --> 00:00:44,693
Sixteen hours without food,
then eight hours to eat.

13
00:00:45,298 --> 00:00:49,458
One guide to it, from 2010, promoted
it for training and fat loss.

14
00:00:50,358 --> 00:00:52,678
The word autophagy isn't in it.

15
00:00:53,183 --> 00:00:54,723
The link came later.

16
00:00:55,443 --> 00:00:57,803
One popular fasting guide says autophagy

17
00:00:58,033 --> 00:01:00,923
"typically begins around
the sixteen-hour mark".

18
00:01:01,622 --> 00:01:03,882
As its source, it links to a study.

19
00:01:04,662 --> 00:01:09,162
A study on mice, which went without food
for twenty-four or forty-eight hours.

20
00:01:09,627 --> 00:01:12,067
That study never measured sixteen hours.

21
00:01:12,147 --> 00:01:14,467
And it wasn't done on people.

22
00:01:15,101 --> 00:01:17,861
Autophagy doesn't start
when you stop eating.

23
00:01:18,561 --> 00:01:22,121
It's running right now in your
cells, at a low background level.

24
00:01:22,665 --> 00:01:24,365
Fasting can turn it up.

25
00:01:24,945 --> 00:01:28,365
But up from what, by how much,
and after how long in a human?

26
00:01:28,845 --> 00:01:30,265
That's the real question.

27
00:01:30,979 --> 00:01:34,459
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

28
00:01:35,079 --> 00:01:36,679
It really helps the channel.

29
00:01:39,069 --> 00:01:41,789
Your cells are full of
machinery that wears out.

30
00:01:42,149 --> 00:01:45,609
Proteins fold the wrong
way. Clumps build up.

31
00:01:45,949 --> 00:01:48,789
Mitochondria, the cell's
power plants, get damaged.

32
00:01:49,516 --> 00:01:52,336
Small things are handled
by the proteasome, a

33
00:01:52,566 --> 00:01:55,816
molecular shredder that
destroys proteins one by one.

34
00:01:56,311 --> 00:01:59,831
Autophagy can take apart
an entire mitochondrion.

35
00:02:00,011 --> 00:02:02,891
The proteasome can't do that on its own.

36
00:02:03,449 --> 00:02:05,089
It starts with a membrane.

37
00:02:05,849 --> 00:02:09,749
A thin sheet forms around the cargo
and keeps growing until it closes.

38
00:02:10,480 --> 00:02:14,600
The result is a bag with a double
wall, called an autophagosome.

39
00:02:15,120 --> 00:02:17,400
Next, it fuses with a lysosome.

40
00:02:17,981 --> 00:02:20,361
A lysosome is the cell's stomach.

41
00:02:21,041 --> 00:02:24,121
A sealed, acidic sac full
of digestive enzymes.

42
00:02:24,991 --> 00:02:27,871
The cargo is broken down
into its building blocks,

43
00:02:27,951 --> 00:02:30,971
and those go back out into
the cell to be used again.

44
00:02:31,572 --> 00:02:37,432
The lysosome's discoverer, Christian de
Duve, gave this process its name in 1963.

45
00:02:38,345 --> 00:02:40,485
How much does a body depend on it?

46
00:02:41,065 --> 00:02:42,665
Newborn mice show it clearly.

47
00:02:43,414 --> 00:02:45,674
At birth, the food supply
from the mother is cut

48
00:02:45,904 --> 00:02:49,774
off, and autophagy surges
in tissues across the body.

49
00:02:50,379 --> 00:02:56,059
Mice born without one key autophagy
gene look normal. They die within a day.

50
00:02:56,485 --> 00:03:00,065
So this isn't an emergency mode
waiting for you to skip a meal.

51
00:03:00,685 --> 00:03:02,765
It's maintenance, and it never stops.

52
00:03:05,375 --> 00:03:09,615
Then where does the fasting idea
come from? Partly from yeast.

53
00:03:10,359 --> 00:03:14,959
In the early 1990s, Yoshinori Ohsumi
took yeast cells that couldn't

54
00:03:15,189 --> 00:03:18,979
digest anything in their recycling
chamber, and starved them.

55
00:03:19,479 --> 00:03:23,859
Within an hour, little bubbles of
cell contents began to pile up inside.

56
00:03:24,639 --> 00:03:27,379
After three hours, they
almost filled the chamber.

57
00:03:28,046 --> 00:03:30,366
Starvation had turned the recycling up.

58
00:03:30,446 --> 00:03:34,486
And because digestion was blocked,
the bubbles had nowhere to go.

59
00:03:35,106 --> 00:03:36,486
Remember that trick.

60
00:03:37,149 --> 00:03:40,769
From those yeast, he found fifteen
genes the process depends on.

61
00:03:41,409 --> 00:03:45,549
That's what the Nobel Prize
was for. Genes, in yeast.

62
00:03:46,029 --> 00:03:47,529
Not a fasting schedule.

63
00:03:49,689 --> 00:03:52,989
The famous fasting results
came later, from mice.

64
00:03:53,929 --> 00:03:58,229
In a 2010 study, young male mice went
without food for one or two days.

65
00:03:58,917 --> 00:04:03,357
In their brain cells, the researchers
counted far more autophagosomes.

66
00:04:03,857 --> 00:04:08,217
After two days, in one type of
neuron, about four times as many.

67
00:04:08,850 --> 00:04:10,310
That's real data.

68
00:04:11,010 --> 00:04:15,750
But a mouse burns about seven times more
energy per gram of body than a human does.

69
00:04:16,393 --> 00:04:19,393
In one lab study, adult
mice lost about fourteen

70
00:04:19,623 --> 00:04:22,853
percent of their weight in
a single day without food.

71
00:04:23,101 --> 00:04:28,881
So for a mouse, a day or two without food
isn't a long fast. It's an extreme one.

72
00:04:29,207 --> 00:04:32,647
And results from different organs
and methods don't always agree.

73
00:04:33,407 --> 00:04:36,587
One mouse study found no
response in the brain at all.

74
00:04:37,187 --> 00:04:40,247
Another, six years later,
found a strong one.

75
00:04:40,721 --> 00:04:44,201
Even in mice, the answer depends
on where you look, and how.

76
00:04:44,641 --> 00:04:47,841
Nobody can turn those days
into a human countdown.

77
00:04:50,731 --> 00:04:52,231
So what about people?

78
00:04:52,871 --> 00:04:54,511
The first problem is simple.

79
00:04:55,091 --> 00:04:58,271
You can't look inside a living
person's organs and watch.

80
00:04:58,980 --> 00:05:04,280
Researchers can take blood, or a small
piece of muscle. That's about it.

81
00:05:04,638 --> 00:05:06,378
And counting isn't enough.

82
00:05:07,018 --> 00:05:10,418
More autophagosomes could mean
the cell is recycling faster...

83
00:05:10,700 --> 00:05:15,360
or that the exit is blocked and they're
piling up, just like in Ohsumi's yeast.

84
00:05:16,044 --> 00:05:19,344
What matters is the whole
flow, from bag to breakdown.

85
00:05:20,044 --> 00:05:21,804
Scientists call it flux.

86
00:05:22,524 --> 00:05:26,464
Measuring it in people is new, and
so far mostly limited to blood.

87
00:05:27,088 --> 00:05:29,608
The results so far are small and mixed.

88
00:05:30,708 --> 00:05:33,188
After a twelve-hour
overnight fast, blood cells

89
00:05:33,418 --> 00:05:36,888
recycled no faster than an
hour after a protein meal.

90
00:05:38,027 --> 00:05:40,287
Eighteen-hour fasts for four
days raised the activity

91
00:05:40,517 --> 00:05:44,147
of one autophagy gene in
blood by about a fifth.

92
00:05:45,107 --> 00:05:48,707
That's a signal from a gene, not
a measurement of the recycling.

93
00:05:49,471 --> 00:05:52,071
The largest study followed a
hundred and twenty-one people

94
00:05:52,301 --> 00:05:56,571
for six months, fasting for
twenty hours, three times a week.

95
00:05:57,140 --> 00:06:00,460
Within the fasting group, the
change wasn't significant.

96
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:05,280
A comparison added after the fact
did find a difference at six months,

97
00:06:05,440 --> 00:06:09,340
partly because autophagy drifted
down in the standard-care group.

98
00:06:09,910 --> 00:06:14,370
One of the field's leading researchers,
Guido Kroemer, was asked directly.

99
00:06:15,210 --> 00:06:19,250
Does a sixteen-hour fast induce
autophagy in any human tissue?

100
00:06:19,928 --> 00:06:22,628
His answer was three
words. "We don't know."

101
00:06:23,909 --> 00:06:26,909
That's the honest state of
things. Not "it doesn't happen".

102
00:06:26,989 --> 00:06:28,629
We simply don't know yet.

103
00:06:31,253 --> 00:06:33,193
So should we even want more of it?

104
00:06:33,713 --> 00:06:35,653
In animals, the case looks strong.

105
00:06:36,343 --> 00:06:39,123
Mice that lose autophagy in
the brain develop movement

106
00:06:39,353 --> 00:06:42,983
problems, and their neurons
fill with clumps of protein.

107
00:06:43,629 --> 00:06:46,229
Turn it up, and some animals live longer.

108
00:06:46,969 --> 00:06:52,289
Mice engineered to overproduce Atg5
lived about seventeen percent longer.

109
00:06:52,857 --> 00:06:55,057
In humans, there's no evidence yet that

110
00:06:55,287 --> 00:06:58,217
boosting autophagy makes
anyone live longer.

111
00:06:59,177 --> 00:07:00,777
And then there's cancer.

112
00:07:01,535 --> 00:07:06,935
Mice with only one working copy of a key
autophagy gene develop tumours more often.

113
00:07:07,515 --> 00:07:10,515
Autophagy helps keep cancer from starting.

114
00:07:10,762 --> 00:07:14,662
But once a tumour is established,
it can use autophagy to survive.

115
00:07:15,762 --> 00:07:20,482
Pancreatic tumours rely on it heavily,
and in mice, blocking it made them shrink.

116
00:07:21,863 --> 00:07:23,343
Autophagy isn't good or bad.

117
00:07:23,823 --> 00:07:27,603
It's a tool, and what it does depends
on whose side it's working for.

118
00:07:30,273 --> 00:07:33,773
So what really happens after
sixteen hours without food?

119
00:07:34,886 --> 00:07:37,686
Fasting does change the
fuel your body uses.

120
00:07:38,486 --> 00:07:41,666
Over time, it relies
more on fat and ketones.

121
00:07:41,859 --> 00:07:45,059
But that's a gradual shift,
not a universal countdown.

122
00:07:46,059 --> 00:07:48,679
And it doesn't tell us
when autophagy changes.

123
00:07:49,487 --> 00:07:53,127
Inside your cells, the recycling
that never stopped keeps going.

124
00:07:53,727 --> 00:07:55,427
Maybe a little faster, in some tissues.

125
00:07:55,657 --> 00:07:58,747
By how much, nobody can tell you yet.

126
00:07:59,156 --> 00:08:01,896
So when a timeline promises
that repair begins at a

127
00:08:02,126 --> 00:08:05,356
precise hour, it knows
more than the science does.

128
00:08:05,993 --> 00:08:09,373
Your cells don't stay alive
by keeping everything forever.

129
00:08:10,079 --> 00:08:13,269
Sometimes, staying alive means
knowing what to throw away.
